{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"tl-v1","tool":"ragflow_retrieval","arguments":{"dataset_ids":["74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37"],"metadata_condition":{"logic":"and","conditions":[{"name":"path","comparison_operator":"is","value":"sources"}]},"question":"\"I don't think we can just drop it\" \"#8014\" \"context\" \"race\" \"closed\"","page_size":10,"vector_similarity_weight":0.3,"similarity_threshold":0.2,"top_k":1024,"keyword":false},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"chunks\": [{\"content\": \"> \\t\\n\\n> r.GET(\\\"/\\\", func(c *gin.Context) {\\n\\n> \\tc.HTML(http.StatusOK, \\\"index.tmpl\\\", gin.H{})\\n\\n> })\\n\\n> ```\\n\\n> \\n\\n> When `myCustomMiddleware` runs, the span has either not started yet, or is already finished. If it's already finished, the context shouldn't have the span within it, as we're not within the trace anymore.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> Right now, if you create a span within `myCustomMiddleware`, after the call to `c.Next()`, it won't be a child of the otelgin span. After your change it is one.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> This is what the defer prevents. While I agree to fixing this race condition, the defer does have a use and I don't think we can just drop it.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> (ideally, we should add a test for this too)\\n\\nAh that’s a really good observation\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T08:34:20Z\\n\\n> The intent of the defer is to remove the span from the context, so spans using the same context aren't childs of the created span once the middleware has finished running.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> Imaging the following setup:\\n\\n> \\n\\n> ```go\\n\\n> r := gin.New()\\n\\n> r.Use(myCustomMiddleware)\\n\\n> r.Use(otelgin.Middleware(\\\"my-server\\\"))\\n\\n> \\t\\n\\n> r.GET(\\\"/\\\", func(c *gin.Context) {\\n\\n> \\tc.HTML(http.StatusOK, \\\"index.tmpl\\\", gin.H{})\\n\\n> })\\n\\n> ```\\n\\n> \\n\\n> When `myCustomMiddleware` runs, the span has either not started yet, or is already finished. If it's already finished, the context shouldn't have the span within it, as we're not within the trace anymore.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> Right now, if you create a span within `myCustomMiddleware`, after the call to `c.Next()`, it won't be a child of the otelgin span. After your change it is one.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> This is what the defer prevents. While I agree to fixing this race condition, the defer does have a use and I don't think we can just drop it.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> (ideally, we should add a test for this too)\\n\\nThat's a really good theory about what the original code was intending to do and you made a really good point. However, I think there are some fundamental issues with this approach:\\n\\n1. This is an anti-pattern for context.Context\\n\\nThe defer modifies c.Request.Context() after passing it forward, which violates Go's context conventions. From the https://pkg.go.dev/context:\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"r get func c gin context c html http statusok index tmpl gin h when mycustommiddlewar run the span ha either not start yet or is alreadi finish if it s alreadi finish the context shouldn t have the span within it a we re not within the trace anymor right now if you creat a span within mycustommiddlewar after the call to c next it won t be a child of the otelgin span after your chang it is one this is what the defer prevent while i agre to fix this race condit the defer doe have a use and i don t think we can just drop it ideal we should add a test for this too ah that s a realli good observ nicksherron 2025 10 14t08 34 20z the intent of the defer is to remov the span from the context so span use the same context aren t child of the creat span onc the middlewar ha finish run imag the follow setup go r gin new r use mycustommiddlewar r use otelgin middlewar my server r get func c gin context c html http statusok index tmpl gin h when mycustommiddlewar run the span ha either not start yet or is alreadi finish if it s alreadi finish the context shouldn t have the span within it a we re not within the trace anymor right now if you creat a span within mycustommiddlewar after the call to c next it won t be a child of the otelgin span after your chang it is one this is what the defer prevent while i agre to fix this race condit the defer doe have a use and i don t think we can just drop it ideal we should add a test for this too that s a realli good theori about what the origin code wa intend to do and you made a realli good point howev i think there are some fundament issu with this approach 1 this is an anti pattern for context context the defer modifi c request context after pass it forward which violat go s context convent from the http pkg go dev context\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"89d4bd453e9caf0c\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.2724905832406835, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.08607484462954784, \"vector_similarity\": 0.70746064, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"I also disagree that folks should do this themselves. Especially since this has been the default behavior for many years now (and the same behavior exists for other instrumentations, such as otelecho).\\n\\nI think this discussion would be better synchronously though. If you're able to, I'd recommend you to join our next SIG meeting (tomorrow at 4pm UTC).\\n\\nhttps://github.com/open-telemetry/community/?tab=readme-ov-file#implementation-sigs\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T08:48:08Z\\n\\n> I disagree with this being a violation of Go's conventions (in the context of the middleware pattern, we don't flow forward. At the end of a middleware, we go back to its parent). I also disagree that folks should do this themselves. Especially since this has been the default behavior for many years now (and the same behavior exists for other instrumentations, such as otelecho).\\n\\n> \\n\\n> I think this discussion would be better synchronously though. If you're able to, I'd recommend you to join our next SIG meeting (tomorrow at 4pm UTC). https://github.com/open-telemetry/community/?tab=readme-ov-file#implementation-sigs\\n\\nOk well one thing is for certain, you all have a much better understanding of the existing patterns and user expectations for this project than I do. It's getting quite late for me so I'll sleep on this and give your points their time to marinate. As far as the SIG meeting goes that's a great idea (and sounds fun too) - unfortunately that's a fairly busy time for me but I'll do my best to make it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T08:59:49Z\\n\\n> Especially since this has been the default behavior for many years now (and the same behavior exists for other instrumentations, such as otelecho).\\n\\nThis is a strong argument, I didn’t realize that was the case.  I wasn’t looking to add a breaking change and I’m glad you pointed it out. I’ll see what I can do to come up with something that maintains the existing semantics while also fixing the race.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**dmathieu** — 2025-10-17T10:41:58Z\\n\\nWe have been discussing this in yesterday's SIG meeting, and have decided this fix is incorrect and the current behavior is the proper one.\\n\\nSee https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/issues/4588#issuecomment-3414917592\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"i also disagre that folk should do this themselv especi sinc this ha been the default behavior for mani year now and the same behavior exist for other instrument such a otelecho i think this discuss would be better synchron though if you re abl to i d recommend you to join our next sig meet tomorrow at 4pm utc http github com open telemetri communiti tab readm ov file implement sig nicksherron 2025 10 14t08 48 08z i disagre with this be a violat of go s convent in the context of the middlewar pattern we don t flow forward at the end of a middlewar we go back to it parent i also disagre that folk should do this themselv especi sinc this ha been the default behavior for mani year now and the same behavior exist for other instrument such a otelecho i think this discuss would be better synchron though if you re abl to i d recommend you to join our next sig meet tomorrow at 4pm utc http github com open telemetri communiti tab readm ov file implement sig ok well one thing is for certain you all have a much better understand of the exist pattern and user expect for this project than i do it s get quit late for me so i ll sleep on this and give your point their time to marin a far a the sig meet go that s a great idea and sound fun too unfortun that s a fair busi time for me but i ll do my best to make it nicksherron 2025 10 14t08 59 49z especi sinc this ha been the default behavior for mani year now and the same behavior exist for other instrument such a otelecho this is a strong argument i didn t realiz that wa the case i wasn t look to add a break chang and i m glad you point it out i ll see what i can do to come up with someth that maintain the exist semant while also fix the race dmathieu 2025 10 17t10 41 58z we have been discuss this in yesterday s sig meet and have decid this fix is incorrect and the current behavior is the proper one see http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib issu 4588 issuecom 3414917592\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"b87d58f69aed9a06\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.26724449930064376, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.0816088275723482, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7003944, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"=====================================\\n\\n+ Hits       11598   11605    +7     \\n\\n+ Misses      2808    2795   -13     \\n\\n  Partials     354     354           \\n\\n```\\n\\n... and [1 file with indirect coverage changes](https://app.codecov.io/gh/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014/indirect-changes?src=pr&el=tree-more&utm_medium=referral&utm_source=github&utm_content=comment&utm_campaign=pr+comments&utm_term=open-telemetry)\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary> :rocket: New features to boost your workflow: </summary>\\n\\n- :snowflake: [Test Analytics](https://docs.codecov.com/docs/test-analytics): Detect flaky tests, report on failures, and find test suite problems.\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n---\\n\\n**dmathieu** — 2025-10-14T07:54:22Z\\n\\nThe intent of the defer is to remove the span from the context, so spans using the same context aren't childs of the created span once the middleware has finished running.\\n\\nImaging the following setup:\\n\\n```golang\\n\\nr := gin.New()\\n\\nr.Use(myCustomMiddleware)\\n\\nr.Use(otelgin.Middleware(\\\"my-server\\\"))\\n\\nr.GET(\\\"/\\\", func(c *gin.Context) {\\n\\n\\tc.HTML(http.StatusOK, \\\"index.tmpl\\\", gin.H{})\\n\\n})\\n\\n```\\n\\nWhen `myCustomMiddleware` runs, the span has either not started yet, or is already finished.\\n\\nIf it's already finished, the context shouldn't have the span within it, as we're not within the trace anymore.\\n\\nRight now, if you create a span within `myCustomMiddleware`, after the call to `c.Next()`, it won't be a child of the otelgin span.\\n\\nAfter your change it is one.\\n\\nThis is what the defer prevents.\\n\\nWhile I agree to fixing this race condition, the defer does have a use and I don't think we can just drop it.\\n\\n(ideally, we should add a test for this too)\\n\\n---\\n\\n**akats7** — 2025-10-14T08:01:45Z\\n\\n> The intent of the defer is to remove the span from the context, so spans using the same context aren't childs of the created span once the middleware has finished running.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> Imaging the following setup:\\n\\n> \\n\\n> ```go\\n\\n> r := gin.New()\\n\\n> r.Use(myCustomMiddleware)\\n\\n> r.Use(otelgin.Middleware(\\\"my-server\\\"))\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"hit 11598 11605 7 miss 2808 2795 13 partial 354 354 and 1 file with indirect coverag chang http app codecov io gh open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 indirect chang src pr el tree more utm _ medium referr utm _ sourc github utm _ content comment utm _ campaign pr comment utm _ term open telemetri detail detail summari rocket new featur to boost your workflow summari snowflak test analyt http doc codecov com doc test analyt detect flaki test report on failur and find test suit problem detail dmathieu 2025 10 14t07 54 22z the intent of the defer is to remov the span from the context so span use the same context aren t child of the creat span onc the middlewar ha finish run imag the follow setup golang r gin new r use mycustommiddlewar r use otelgin middlewar my server r get func c gin context c html http statusok index tmpl gin h when mycustommiddlewar run the span ha either not start yet or is alreadi finish if it s alreadi finish the context shouldn t have the span within it a we re not within the trace anymor right now if you creat a span within mycustommiddlewar after the call to c next it won t be a child of the otelgin span after your chang it is one this is what the defer prevent while i agre to fix this race condit the defer doe have a use and i don t think we can just drop it ideal we should add a test for this too akats7 2025 10 14t08 01 45z the intent of the defer is to remov the span from the context so span use the same context aren t child of the creat span onc the middlewar ha finish run imag the follow setup go r gin new r use mycustommiddlewar r use otelgin middlewar my server\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"dfdecac4ba6df693\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.26461349736060397, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.07714281051514857, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7020451, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"  ctx := cfg.Propagators.Extract(savedCtx, ...)\\n\\n  // After: Use context directly (correct)\\n\\n  reqCtx := c.Request.Context()\\n\\n  ctx := cfg.Propagators.Extract(reqCtx, ...)\\n\\n```\\n\\n  The behavior is identical except:\\n\\n  -  No race condition\\n\\n  -  Better performance\\n\\n  -  Simpler code\\n\\n  -  Tests pass with -race\\n\\n  Conclusion\\n\\n  This is a straightforward bug fix that:\\n\\n  1. Removes unnecessary code that causes a race\\n\\n  2. Improves performance (fewer allocations, no defer overhead)\\n\\n  3. Fixes a 2-year-old issue (#4588)\\n\\n  4. Follows precedent from previous race fixes in this project\\n\\n  5. Enables CI to enforce race-free code\\n\\n  There is no downside to this change, and significant benefits. I respectfully request approval to merge.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**akats7** — 2025-10-14T04:58:07Z\\n\\nHi @nicksherron, \\n\\nIt's a fair question, I also don't really see the benefit of it, I'm going to assume it was added for hygiene to avoid leaking the modifications out of the intended request's middleware chain.\\n\\n@flc1125 what are your thoughts, even if it doesn't provide thread safety it does seem valid\\n\\n---\\n\\n**flc1125** — 2025-10-14T05:01:16Z\\n\\n> I'm going to assume it was added for hygiene to avoid leaking the modifications out of the intended request's middleware chain.\\n\\nI agree with this situation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T05:06:09Z\\n\\nI'm confused about this \\\"doesn't provide thread safety\\\" claim. I'm starring at a test failure due to data race when running `go test -race`  in one of my projects that clearly points the finger to the code I removed as being the culprit. When I do a `go mod -replace` using my change in this PR the race goes away. \\n\\n---\\n\\n**flc1125** — 2025-10-14T05:09:52Z\\n\\n> I'm confused about this \\\"doesn't provide thread safety\\\" claim. I'm starring at a test failure due to data race when running `go test -race` in one of my projects that clearly points the finger to the code I removed as being the culprit. When I do a `go mod -replace` using my change in this PR the race goes away.\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"ctx cfg propag extract savedctx after use context direct correct reqctx c request context ctx cfg propag extract reqctx the behavior is ident except no race condit better perform simpler code test pas with race conclus this is a straightforward bug fix that 1 remov unnecessari code that caus a race 2 improv perform fewer alloc no defer overhead 3 fix a2 year old issu 4588 4 follow preced from previous race fix in this project 5 enabl ci to enforc race free code there is no downsid to this chang and signific benefit i respect request approv to merg akats7 2025 10 14t04 58 07z hi nicksherron it s a fair question i also don t realli see the benefit of it i m go to assum it wa ad for hygien to avoid leak the modif out of the intend request s middlewar chain flc1125 what are your thought even if it doesn t provid thread safeti it doe seem valid flc1125 2025 10 14t05 01 16z i m go to assum it wa ad for hygien to avoid leak the modif out of the intend request s middlewar chain i agre with this situat nicksherron 2025 10 14t05 06 09z i m confus about this doesn t provid thread safeti claim i m star at a test failur due to data race when run go test race in one of my project that clear point the finger to the code i remov a be the culprit when i do a go mod replac use my chang in this pr the race go away flc1125 2025 10 14t05 09 52z i m confus about this doesn t provid thread safeti claim i m star at a test failur due to data race when run go test race in one of my project that clear point the finger to the code i remov a be the culprit when i do a go mod replac use my chang in this pr the race go away\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"cad80d2ecd6e532f\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.26454263506184295, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.08151950723120423, \"vector_similarity\": 0.6915966, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"\\t\\t)\\n\\n\\t\\tc.Next()\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n}\\n\\nfunc haveDeferMiddleware() gin.HandlerFunc {\\n\\n\\treturn func(c *gin.Context) {\\n\\n\\t\\torigCtx := c.Request.Context()\\n\\n\\t\\tdefer func() {\\n\\n\\t\\t\\tc.Request = c.Request.WithContext(origCtx)\\n\\n\\t\\t}()\\n\\n\\t\\tc.Request.WithContext(\\n\\n\\t\\t\\tcontext.WithValue(origCtx, \\\"key1\\\", \\\"value1\\\"),\\n\\n\\t\\t)\\n\\n\\t\\tc.Next()\\n\\n\\t}\\n\\n}\\n\\n```\\n\\n</details>\\n\\nWhether `defer` is present or not, data races have always existed. My understanding of this is that the reason lies in the fact that `*http.Request` itself cannot guarantee thread safety, and this applies to any framework, including the native one as well.\\n\\nTherefore, I'm not quite clear about what issue you are trying to verify.\\n\\n[As mentioned before](https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/issues/4588#issuecomment-2490995125), I was wondering what this piece of code is intended to handle—it doesn't seem to serve any purpose and appears to be removable.\\n\\n```go\\n\\ndefer func() {\\n\\n\\tc.Request = c.Request.WithContext(savedCtx)\\n\\n}()\\n\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T06:52:09Z\\n\\n> Whether `defer` is present or not, data races have always existed. My understanding of this is that the reason lies in the fact that `*http.Request` itself cannot guarantee thread safety, and this applies to any framework, including the native one as well.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> Therefore, I'm not quite clear about what issue you are trying to verify.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> [As mentioned before](https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/issues/4588#issuecomment-2490995125), I was wondering what this piece of code is intended to handle—it doesn't seem to serve any purpose and appears to be removable.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> ```go\\n\\n> defer func() {\\n\\n> \\tc.Request = c.Request.WithContext(savedCtx)\\n\\n> }()\\n\\n> ```\\n\\nI feel like we're talking in circles a little here and I don't know if I can explain it any better, but I'll try. \\n\\nI'm not claiming this change will make `*http.Request` impervious to data races. The disconnect seems to be thinking we're adding concurrency safety. **We're removing the code that breaks it**. \\n\\nI think there's a misunderstanding about when the defer runs. Let me clarify:\\n\\nTimeline with OLD code (defer pattern):\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"c next func havedefermiddlewar gin handlerfunc return func c gin context origctx c request context defer func c request c request withcontext origctx c request withcontext context withvalu origctx key1 value1 c next detail whether defer is present or not data race have alway exist my understand of this is that the reason lie in the fact that http request itself can not guarante thread safeti and this appli to ani framework includ the nativ one a well therefor i m not quit clear about what issu you are tri to verifi a mention befor http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib issu 4588 issuecom 2490995125 i wa wonder what this piec of code is intend to handl it doesn t seem to serv ani purpos and appear to be remov go defer func c request c request withcontext savedctx nicksherron 2025 10 14t06 52 09z whether defer is present or not data race have alway exist my understand of this is that the reason lie in the fact that http request itself can not guarante thread safeti and this appli to ani framework includ the nativ one a well therefor i m not quit clear about what issu you are tri to verifi a mention befor http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib issu 4588 issuecom 2490995125 i wa wonder what this piec of code is intend to handl it doesn t seem to serv ani purpos and appear to be remov go defer func c request c request withcontext savedctx i feel like we re talk in circl a littl here and i don t know if i can explain it ani better but i ll tri i m not claim this chang will make http request impervi to data race the disconnect seem to be think we re ad concurr safeti we re remov the code that break it i think there s a misunderstand about when the defer run let me clarifi timelin with old code defer pattern\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"d20c067ac2a8853b\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.263792097360604, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.07714281051514857, \"vector_similarity\": 0.6993071, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"**Source ID**: [SRC-PR-07]\\n\\n# Pull Request: otelgin: remove unneeded context save/restore to avoid data race (open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib#8014)\\n\\n**Canonical URL**: https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014  \\n\\n**Author**: nicksherron  \\n\\n**Created**: 2025-10-14T04:01:24Z · **Updated**: 2025-10-17T10:42:07Z  \\n\\n**Collected**: 2026-07-14T14:35:41Z  \\n\\n**State**: closed  \\n\\n**Branch**: `fix/otelgin-data-race` → `main`  \\n\\n**Changes**: +83 / -10 across 2 files  \\n\\n**Labels**: —  \\n\\n**Milestone**: —\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThere seems to be absolutely no reason to save and restore the request context in a deferred function. The way it was done before could (and did) lead to a race condition because when c.Next() is called, the request context could be changed by another goroutine, and then the deferred function would modify the request while another goroutine is using it. \\n\\nI can't for the life of me figure out what benefit the previous code was trying to achieve, other than maybe trying to minimize the context size in memory, but that seems unnecessary and not worth the risk of a data race. Furthermore, I'm not even sure that said memory savings would pay off because when running `go build -gcflags=\\\"-m\\\"` against the current implementation you can see that context and request escapes to heap:\\n\\n```\\n\\n./gin.go:84:37: \\\"nil context\\\" escapes to heap\\n\\n./gin.go:84:37: new(http.Request) escapes to heap\\n\\n```\\n\\nThis is likely negligible but I figured I'd add it for more context, no pun intended 😉\\n\\nPerhaps the goal was not that of performance/memory savings but instead to \\\"restore\\\" the context. If so, this is also incorrect because in Gin, there's no need to restore the context - each request gets its own *gin.Context .\\n\\nFinally, this change actually more closely matches the [code cited](https://github.com/DataDog/dd-trace-go/blob/8fb554ff7cf694267f9077ae35e27ce4689ed8b6/contrib/gin-gonic/gin/gintrace.go) as the \\\"inspiration\\\" for the otelgin package. \\n\\nWithout this change, we unfortunately cannot run our tests with the race detector enabled and is not production ready due to not being goroutine safe.\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"sourc id src pr 07 pull request otelgin remov unneed context save restor to avoid data race open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib 8014 canon url http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 author nicksherron creat 2025 10 14t04 01 24z updat 2025 10 17t10 42 07z collect 2026 07 14t14 35 41z state close branch fix otelgin data race main chang 83 10 across 2 file label mileston descript there seem to be absolut no reason to save and restor the request context in a defer function the way it wa done befor could and did lead to a race condit becaus when c next is call the request context could be chang by anoth goroutin and then the defer function would modifi the request while anoth goroutin is use it i can t for the life of me figur out what benefit the previous code wa tri to achiev other than mayb tri to minim the context size in memori but that seem unnecessari and not worth the risk of a data race furthermor i m not even sure that said memori save would pay off becaus when run go build gcflag m against the current implement you can see that context and request escap to heap gin go 84 37 nil context escap to heap gin go 84 37 new http request escap to heap this is like neglig but i figur i d add it for more context no pun intend perhap the goal wa not that of perform memori save but instead to restor the context if so this is also incorrect becaus in gin there s no need to restor the context each request get it own gin context final this chang actual more close match the code cite http github com datadog dd trace go blob 8fb554ff7cf694267f9077ae35e27ce4689ed8b6 contrib gin gonic gin gintrac go a the inspir for the otelgin packag without this chang we unfortun can not run our test with the race detector enabl and is not product readi due to not be goroutin safe\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"a69b1eaa6d5ba704\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.260640819360604, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.07714281051514857, \"vector_similarity\": 0.68880284, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"> \\\"Contexts form a tree... Successive calls to WithValue, WithCancel, WithDeadline, or WithTimeout derive new contexts.\\\"\\n\\nContexts are meant to flow forward, not be \\\"restored\\\" backward. The library shouldn't enforce this opinion.\\n\\n2. Most users want otelgin early in the chain\\n\\nI (and I assume most users) place otelgin as one of the first middleware specifically so child spans can be created from it:\\n\\nr.Use(otelgin.Middleware(\\\"my-service\\\"))  // ← First - creates root span\\n\\nr.Use(loggingMiddleware)                  // ← Creates child spans\\n\\nr.Use(authMiddleware)                     // ← Creates child spans\\n\\nr.GET(\\\"/\\\", handler)                       // ← Handler creates child spans\\n\\nCommon use cases that need the parent span:\\n\\n- Database transactions with tracing\\n\\n- Inter-service HTTP calls with trace propagation\\n\\n- Structured logging with trace IDs\\n\\n- Background jobs that need trace context\\n\\nYour example breaks all of these - they'd have no parent span.\\n\\n3. Users can control span isolation themselves \\n\\nIf a user doesn't want their spans to be children of otelgin's span, they can do exactly what the defer does - but explicitly:\\n\\n```go\\n\\nvar tracer = otel.Tracer(instrumentationName)\\n\\nfunc myCustomMiddleware(c *gin.Context) {\\n\\n    ctx, span := tracer.Start(c.Request.Context(), \\\"myCustomMiddleware\\\")\\n\\n    defer span.End()\\n\\n    c.Next() // runs otelgin and other middleware\\n\\n    // Explicitly restore context if the user doesn't want child spans and since the user is in control of goroutines the onus is on them to do so safely (as it should be)  \\n\\n    c.Request = c.Request.WithContext(ctx)\\n\\n}\\n\\n```\\n\\nThis gives users control without the library making opinionated (and race-prone) decisions.\\n\\nEven if we agreed on the semantics, I'm not sure of a safe way of doing it from a library implementation.\\n\\nBottom line: The defer enforces an anti-pattern, breaks the common use case (otelgin as root span), prevents valid patterns (goroutines with trace context), and causes data races.\\n\\nUsers who need span isolation can manage it themselves without the library making unsafe assumptions.\\n\\nWhat do you think? I'm happy to add documentation showing how users can achieve span isolation if they need it. \\n\\n---\\n\\n**dmathieu** — 2025-10-14T08:42:01Z\\n\\nI disagree with this being a violation of Go's conventions (in the context of the middleware pattern, we don't flow forward. At the end of a middleware, we go back to its parent).\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"context form a tree success call to withvalu withcancel withdeadlin or withtimeout deriv new context context are meant to flow forward not be restor backward the librari shouldn t enforc this opinion 2 most user want otelgin earli in the chain i and i assum most user place otelgin a one of the first middlewar specif so child span can be creat from it r use otelgin middlewar my servic first creat root span r use loggingmiddlewar creat child span r use authmiddlewar creat child span r get handler handler creat child span common use case that need the parent span databas transact with trace inter servic http call with trace propag structur log with trace id background job that need trace context your exampl break all of these they d have no parent span 3 user can control span isol themselv if a user doesn t want their span to be child of otelgin s span they can do exact what the defer doe but explicit go var tracer otel tracer instrumentationnam func mycustommiddlewar c gin context ctx span tracer start c request context mycustommiddlewar defer span end c next run otelgin and other middlewar explicit restor context if the user doesn t want child span and sinc the user is in control of goroutin the onus is on them to do so safe a it should be c request c request withcontext ctx this give user control without the librari make opinion and race prone decis even if we agre on the semant i m not sure of a safe way of do it from a librari implement bottom line the defer enforc an anti pattern break the common use case otelgin a root span prevent valid pattern goroutin with trace context and caus data race user who need span isol can manag it themselv without the librari make unsaf assumpt what do you think i m happi to add document show how user can achiev span isol if they need it dmathieu 2025 10 14t08 42 01z i disagre with this be a violat of go s convent in the context of the middlewar pattern we don t flow forward at the end of a middlewar we go back to it parent\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"9ae5e4f55cbf7762\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.2547772614805245, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.06821077640074931, \"vector_similarity\": 0.69009906, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"**Source ID**: [SRC-PR-07]\\n\\n# Pull Request: otelgin: remove unneeded context save/restore to avoid data race (open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib#8014)\\n\\n**Canonical URL**: https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014  \\n\\n**Author**: nicksherron  \\n\\n**Created**: 2025-10-14T04:01:24Z · **Updated**: 2025-10-17T10:42:07Z  \\n\\n**Collected**: 2026-07-14T14:35:41Z  \\n\\n**State**: closed  \\n\\n**Branch**: `fix/otelgin-data-race` → `main`  \\n\\n**Changes**: +83 / -10 across 2 files  \\n\\n**Labels**: —  \\n\\n**Milestone**: —\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Users Cannot Fix This Themselves\\n\\n This is middleware code that users cannot work around without forking the library. The race happens inside otelgin.Middleware(). A user who wants to use otelgin AND pass race detection has no choice but to:\\n\\n  1. Fork this library\\n\\n  2. Apply this exact fix\\n\\n  3. Maintain their fork indefinitely\\n\\n  This is not \\\"leaving the decision to users\\\"—it's forcing users to fork or live with failing race detection.\\n\\n**Source ID**: [SRC-PR-07]\\n\\n# Pull Request: otelgin: remove unneeded context save/restore to avoid data race (open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib#8014)\\n\\n**Canonical URL**: https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014  \\n\\n**Author**: nicksherron  \\n\\n**Created**: 2025-10-14T04:01:24Z · **Updated**: 2025-10-17T10:42:07Z  \\n\\n**Collected**: 2026-07-14T14:35:41Z  \\n\\n**State**: closed  \\n\\n**Branch**: `fix/otelgin-data-race` → `main`  \\n\\n**Changes**: +83 / -10 across 2 files  \\n\\n**Labels**: —  \\n\\n**Milestone**: —\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Precedent: This Project Has Fixed Similar Races Before\\n\\n  From the git history:\\n\\n  - #4577: \\\"otelgrpc: Fix data race in stats handlers\\\" (merged)\\n\\n  - #881: \\\"otelsarama: Fix WrapAsyncProducer race condition\\\" (merged)\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"sourc id src pr 07 pull request otelgin remov unneed context save restor to avoid data race open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib 8014 canon url http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 author nicksherron creat 2025 10 14t04 01 24z updat 2025 10 17t10 42 07z collect 2026 07 14t14 35 41z state close branch fix otelgin data race main chang 83 10 across 2 file label mileston user can not fix this themselv this is middlewar code that user can not work around without fork the librari the race happen insid otelgin middlewar a user who want to use otelgin and pas race detect ha no choic but to 1 fork this librari 2 appli this exact fix 3 maintain their fork indefinit this is not leav the decis to user it s forc user to fork or live with fail race detect sourc id src pr 07 pull request otelgin remov unneed context save restor to avoid data race open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib 8014 canon url http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 author nicksherron creat 2025 10 14t04 01 24z updat 2025 10 17t10 42 07z collect 2026 07 14t14 35 41z state close branch fix otelgin data race main chang 83 10 across 2 file label mileston preced this project ha fix similar race befor from the git histori 4577 otelgrpc fix data race in stat handler merg 881 otelsarama fix wrapasyncproduc race condit merg\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"c020b4557f49eb79\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.25276118612284343, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.05910010160406207, \"vector_similarity\": 0.70463705, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"What about implementing an similar hot/cold storage?\\n\\n**dashpole** on `sdk/metric/exemplar/storage.go` — 2025-10-03T16:12:32Z\\n\\nThere is still a global ordering for Offer vs Collect calls.  Consider a histogram with a single bucket.  Each Offer call that updates the atomic.Value prior to the Load() call in Collect can be considered to have happened in the previous collection interval.  Each Offer call that updates the atomic.Value after the Load() call can be considered to have happened in the next collection interval.  That principle still holds true for histograms with multiple buckets.\\n\\nI don't think the complexity of hot/cold storage is necessary (at least not for histogram reservoirs)\\n\\n**MrAlias** on `sdk/metric/exemplar/storage.go` — 2025-10-03T16:34:09Z\\n\\nI think that works for a single bucket. But for the case of multiple buckets this could report values for subsequent buckets that occurred after a \\\"collection cycle\\\" for a previous bucket ended. I don't think a collection cycle is defined on a per-bucket basis, right?\\n\\n**dashpole** on `sdk/metric/exemplar/storage.go` — 2025-10-03T17:34:51Z\\n\\nThe race that this allows is the following:\\n\\n```golang\\n\\n// Record a measurement in bucket A\\n\\nmyHist.Record(ctx, 1)\\n\\n// Record a measurement in bucket B\\n\\nmyHist.Record(ctx, 10000)\\n\\n```\\n\\nIn this case, it is possible for the measurement in bucket B to be included in a collection interval, but the measurement in bucket A is not included.  That would happen if we collect bucket A, then measure to buckets A and B, then collect bucket B.\\n\\nA similar case exists for https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go/pull/7427 as well.  Doing:\\n\\n```golang\\n\\n// Record a measurement with attribute set a\\n\\nmyCounter.Add(ctx, 1, a)\\n\\n// Record a measurement with attribute set b\\n\\nmyCounter.Add(ctx, 10000, b)\\n\\n```\\n\\nThe above can result in the add to attribute set `b` being included in collect, but attribute set `a` is not, since we do not globally prevent measurements during collection, and we don't iterate over attribute sets in a predictable order.\\n\\nThe second race (with different attribute sets) seems worse than the exemplar case, IMO, and I think both are acceptable.\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"what about implement an similar hot cold storag dashpol on sdk metric exemplar storag go 2025 10 03t16 12 32z there is still a global order for offer v collect call consid a histogram with a singl bucket each offer call that updat the atom valu prior to the load call in collect can be consid to have happen in the previous collect interv each offer call that updat the atom valu after the load call can be consid to have happen in the next collect interv that principl still hold true for histogram with multipl bucket i don t think the complex of hot cold storag is necessari at least not for histogram reservoir mralia on sdk metric exemplar storag go 2025 10 03t16 34 09z i think that work for a singl bucket but for the case of multipl bucket this could report valu for subsequ bucket that occur after a collect cycl for a previous bucket end i don t think a collect cycl is defin on a per bucket basi right dashpol on sdk metric exemplar storag go 2025 10 03t17 34 51z the race that this allow is the follow golang record a measur in bucket a myhist record ctx 1 record a measur in bucket b myhist record ctx 10000 in this case it is possibl for the measur in bucket b to be includ in a collect interv but the measur in bucket ais not includ that would happen if we collect bucket a then measur to bucket a and b then collect bucket b a similar case exist for http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go pull 7427 a well do golang record a measur with attribut set a mycount add ctx 1a record a measur with attribut set b mycount add ctx 10000 b the abov can result in the add to attribut set b be includ in collect but attribut set ais not sinc we do not global prevent measur dure collect and we don t iter over attribut set in a predict order the second race with differ attribut set seem wors than the exemplar case imo and i think both are accept\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8757264a8a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-7443.md\", \"id\": \"723288284d7818be\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.24763138054341471, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.06279220077630672, \"vector_similarity\": 0.6789228, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-7443.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8757264a8a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-7443.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-7443.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 20034, \"chunk_count\": 14, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:06\", \"token_count\": 7244, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"**Source ID**: [SRC-PR-07]\\n\\n# Pull Request: otelgin: remove unneeded context save/restore to avoid data race (open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib#8014)\\n\\n**Canonical URL**: https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014  \\n\\n**Author**: nicksherron  \\n\\n**Created**: 2025-10-14T04:01:24Z · **Updated**: 2025-10-17T10:42:07Z  \\n\\n**Collected**: 2026-07-14T14:35:41Z  \\n\\n**State**: closed  \\n\\n**Branch**: `fix/otelgin-data-race` → `main`  \\n\\n**Changes**: +83 / -10 across 2 files  \\n\\n**Labels**: —  \\n\\n**Milestone**: —\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Discussion (27)\\n\\n**linux-foundation-easycla[bot]** — 2025-10-14T04:01:31Z\\n\\n<a href=\\\"https://easycla.lfx.linuxfoundation.org/#/?version=2\\\"><img src=\\\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/cla-project-logo-prod/cla-signed.svg?v=2\\\" alt=\\\"CLA Signed\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" height=\\\"28\\\" width=\\\"328\\\" ></a><br/><br />The committers listed above are authorized under a signed CLA.<ul><li>:white_check_mark: login: nicksherron / name: Nick Sherron  (43d6c1b5f0c27c73a83543d0f681e2dc766d86dc, 6c560fe0a618c400f5961126b3daf71074a98113, ebe29b975966edee33e7c0e2fdcca7c89710fd63)</li></ul>\\n\\n---\\n\\n**flc1125** — 2025-10-14T04:35:20Z\\n\\nThe current changes do not guarantee thread safety.\\n\\nAdditionally, I lean towards the view that for infrastructure-level components or frameworks, there is no need to consider the concurrency safety of `req.WithContext` and `req.Context()`.\\n\\nOnce these components introduce certain mechanisms for concurrency safety, it may lead to performance degradation.\\n\\nFor users pursuing high performance, such performance degradation is unacceptable. Moreover, once implemented, users have no choice but to use these mechanisms.\\n\\nTherefore, I prefer to leave the decision of whether to ensure concurrency safety to the users themselves, and let them implement these mechanisms on their own.\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"sourc id src pr 07 pull request otelgin remov unneed context save restor to avoid data race open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib 8014 canon url http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 author nicksherron creat 2025 10 14t04 01 24z updat 2025 10 17t10 42 07z collect 2026 07 14t14 35 41z state close branch fix otelgin data race main chang 83 10 across 2 file label mileston discuss 27 linux foundat easycla bot 2025 10 14t04 01 31z a href http easycla lfx linuxfound org version 2 img src http s3 amazonaw com cla project logo prod cla sign svg v2 alt cla sign align left height 28 width 328 a br br the committ list abov are author under a sign cla ul li white _ check _ mark login nicksherron name nick sherron 43d6c1b5f0c27c73a83543d0f681e2dc766d86dc 6c560fe0a618c400f5961126b3daf71074a98113 ebe29b975966edee33e7c0e2fdcca7c89710fd63 li ul flc1125 2025 10 14t04 35 20z the current chang do not guarante thread safeti addit i lean toward the view that for infrastructur level compon or framework there is no need to consid the concurr safeti of req withcontext and req context onc these compon introduc certain mechan for concurr safeti it may lead to perform degrad for user pursu high perform such perform degrad is unaccept moreov onc implement user have no choic but to use these mechan therefor i prefer to leav the decis of whether to ensur concurr safeti to the user themselv and let them implement these mechan on their own\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"9d57dc1fb2041765\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.24752631724276397, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.050168067489662814, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7080289, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}], \"pagination\": {\"page\": 1, \"page_size\": 10, \"total_chunks\": 70, \"total_pages\": 7}, \"query_info\": {\"question\": \"\\\"I don't think we can just drop it\\\" \\\"#8014\\\" \\\"context\\\" \\\"race\\\" \\\"closed\\\"\", \"similarity_threshold\": 0.2, \"vector_weight\": 0.3, \"keyword_search\": false, \"dataset_count\": 1}}"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"PR #8014 was closed because the SIG concluded that removing the deferred context restoration was semantically incorrect, even though it eliminated the reproduced race.\n\nThe defer intentionally removes the otelgin span from the request context after the middleware returns. That preserves the established behavior: middleware that resumes after `c.Next()` should not create spans that are children of a span which has already finished. Removing it would change that behavior—and would be a breaking change from the long-standing pattern also used by other instrumentation such as `otelecho`.\n\nAfter discussing it in the SIG meeting, maintainers decided the existing behavior was the correct one and closed the PR rather than merge it. [SRC-PR-07: PR #8014](https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014)"}}
